		heading: `Who is speaking, and to whom`,
		bodyHtml: `<p>Proverbs 3 opens as a father talking to his son: &ldquo;My son, do not forget my teaching, but let your heart keep my commandments,&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:1). Verse 2 gives the first reason: &ldquo;for they will add length of days and years of life and peace to you.&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:2). The two verses most people know, 5 and 6, are the center of that conversation, not a stand-alone slogan.</p><p>Notice what comes before them. The father asks for faithfulness: &ldquo;Do not let covenant love and faithfulness leave you; bind them around your neck, write them on the tablet of your heart.&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:3). Trust in verse 5 grows out of a heart already shaped by love and faithfulness.`,
	},
	{
		id: 'verses-5-6',
		heading: `Verses 5-6: trust, then know`,
		bodyHtml: `<p>&ldquo;Trust in our Father with all your heart, and do not lean on your own understanding.&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:5). &ldquo;In all your ways know him, and he will make your paths straight.&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:6). Each verse has a command and its other side: trust with all your heart, and do not lean on yourself; know him in all your ways, and he makes the paths straight.</p><p>The verb in verse 6 is the Hebrew <em>yada</em>, to know. It is the word for knowing a person, not for agreeing with a fact. The father is not asking his son to tick a box about God in every area of life. He is asking him to walk through every area of life with our Father, and to know him there.`,
	},
